mbling letter, Ansonius noted that when he was at college, \" … if a man passed an old acquaintance wittingly, without recognizing him, he was said— ‘To cut him.’\" Ansonius then went on to explain the performance of the cut and noted that for a time the term \"to spear\" was used instead of to cut. However, that term did not remain long in use, and this act was generally known as \"the cut\" ever after.", "reference": "27 September 2013, Kane, Kathryn, The Regency Redingote Blog The Cut: The Ultimate & Final Social Weapon"}], "synonyms": ["blank", "cut"], "antonyms": [], "translations": [], "subsenses": []}], "synonyms": [], "antonyms": []}, {"language": {"code": "en", "name": "English"}, "partOfSpeech": "adjective", "pronunciations": [{"type": "ipa", "text": "/spɪə̯(ɹ)/", "tags": ["UK"]}, {"type": "ipa", "text": "/spɪɹ/", "tags": ["Canada", "US"]}], "forms": [], "senses": [{"definition": "Male.", "tags": ["not comparable"], "examples": ["a spear counterpart"], "quotes": [{"text": "When I was young, I was so desperate I'd go looking on the spear side.", "reference": "2018, A Very English Scandal (TV series), episode 1:"}], "synonyms": [], "antonyms": [], "translations": [], "subsenses": []}, {"definition": "Pertaining to male family members.", "tags": ["not comparable"], "examples": ["the spear side of the family"], "quotes": [], "synonyms": [], "antonyms": [], "translations": [], "subsenses": []}], "synonyms": [], "antonyms": ["distaff"]}, {"language": {"code": "en", "name": "English"}, "partOfSpeech": "noun", "pronunciations": [{"type": "ipa", "text": "/spɪə̯(ɹ)/", "tags": ["UK"]}, {"type": "ipa", "text": "/spɪɹ/", "tags": ["Canada", "US"]}], "forms": [{"word": "spears", "tags": ["plural"]}], "senses": [{"definition": "(botany) The sprout of a plant, stalk", "tags": [], "examples": [], "quotes": [], "synonyms": [], "antonyms": [], "translations": [], "subsenses": []}, {"definition": "(obsolete) A church spire.", "tags": ["obsolete"], "examples": [], "quotes": [],